      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pasir Ris Town derives its name from the long stretch of white sandy beach along Singapore’s northeast coast facing Pulau Ubin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasir_Ris"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1958, the Singapore Rural Board revamped Pasir Ris beaches, turning the area from an elite retreat into a public seaside destination.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sg101.gov.sg/resources/archives/ourneighbourhood-pasir-ris/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Pasir Ris Heritage Trail, featuring 27 heritage sites and seven markers, traces the history of kampong communities and the town’s beachside charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sg101.gov.sg/resources/archives/ourneighbourhood-pasir-ris/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first HDB blocks appeared in 1986, bringing an MRT station and market areas that helped attract residents to the new town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://familiesforlife.sg/pages/Activity/Discover-SG-Pasir-Ris-Community-Heritage"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pasir Ris will become a Cross Island line interchange by 2030, adding new stations Loyang and Pasir Ris East to the existing MRT network.",
